<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · Maritime Southeast Asia</p><h1 class="st-title">Kingdom of Mengwi</h1><p class="st-meta">1700 CE – 1891 CE (approx.) · capital Mengwi</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Powerful 18th-century kingdom of southern Bali, builder of the moated royal temple of Taman Ayun, destroyed by its neighbours in 1891.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Mengwi was founded around 1700 by I Gusti Agung Putu, a noble who broke away from the declining <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-gelgel">Gelgel</a> order and built a new realm in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a> lands of south-central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bali">Bali</a>. Through war and marriage alliances it became one of the strongest Balinese states of the 18th century.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The rulers dominated an extensive network of irrigation societies and temple congregations, and their state temple, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pura-taman-ayun">Pura Taman Ayun</a>, surrounded by moats, expressed royal claims to fertility and order. In the 18th century Mengwi also held <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/blambangan-kingdom">Blambangan</a> in eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/java">Java</a> for a time. Power depended on shifting loyalties of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> lords.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Rivalries with neighbouring courts wore the kingdom down, and in 1891 a coalition of neighbouring courts led by the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-badung">Kingdom of Badung</a> and the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-tabanan">Kingdom of Tabanan</a> conquered Mengwi and divided its lands. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pura-taman-ayun">Taman Ayun</a> is now part of a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage</a> cultural landscape.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="kingdom, 1700 CE – 1891 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M22 72 V40 L36 54 L50 28 L64 54 L78 40 V72 Z"/><path d="M22 80 H78"/><circle cx="50" cy="24" r="3"/><circle cx="22" cy="36" r="2.5"/><circle cx="78" cy="36" r="2.5"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">kingdom</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Maritime Southeast Asia</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:93.53%;width:3.80%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1700 CE – 1891 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Mengwi</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>I Gusti Agung Putu</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Balinese Hinduism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Balinese</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/indonesia">Indonesia</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Mengwi</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1796&amp;focus=kingdom-of-mengwi" aria-label="Kingdom of Mengwi on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7862 2497 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Kingdom of Mengwi lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M8182 2753L8152 2721M8182 2753L8183 2757"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M8152 2721h0M8183 2757h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M8182 2753h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M8182 2753h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Mengwi. <a href="/atlas?year=1796&amp;focus=kingdom-of-mengwi">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

